+# Specialty-Narrowed Veterinarian Reads Are Not Cached
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+Both read methods on `VetRepository` are cached under the single `vets` cache. That cache is created through the JCache API with statistics enabled and nothing else — no size limit, no expiry, no eviction. The source comment in `CacheConfiguration` records that a size limit would have to come from the chosen provider's own configuration, which the application does not supply.
+
+`REQ-VET-004` adds a caller-supplied specialty name to both published forms of the veterinarian directory. Spring's default key generator builds a cache key from a method's arguments, so caching a narrowed read would key entries on a string that any unauthenticated caller chooses, in a cache that never gives an entry back.
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Cache the narrowed reads like their un-narrowed siblings.** Rejected: every distinct spelling a caller sends — matching a specialty or not — would retain a result set for the life of the process, in a cache with no bound. Consistency of annotation buys a memory-growth path that scales with caller input.
+2. **Cache them and bound the cache.** Rejected for this slice: bounding requires provider-specific configuration and an eviction policy. That is the standing cache question in [system-design.md](../system-design.md#open-questions-from-the-survey), not this requirement.
+3. **Leave the narrowed reads uncached** (chosen).
+
+## Decision
+
+The specialty-narrowed read methods on `VetRepository` carry no cache annotation. The un-narrowed read methods keep theirs unchanged, so a request without the parameter is served exactly as it is today. A narrowed request reaches the database each time.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- The default directory response — page form and machine-readable form alike — is untouched, including its cache behavior.
+- A narrowed request costs one query. The veterinarian table is a handful of rows, and the narrowing runs as a single joined query with the paging applied in SQL.
+- The repository reads asymmetrically: two cached methods beside two uncached ones. This ADR is the answer to that question.
+- The pre-existing paged read stays keyed by a caller-supplied page number, unbounded in the same way over a much narrower key space. That is baseline, recorded under Open Questions, and not changed here.
+- Reversal is one annotation per method, once the cache carries a size bound and an eviction policy.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Requirements:** REQ-VET-004
+
+- [Contracts — the veterinarian directory routes](../system-design.md#contracts) — the two narrowed routes and their parameter contract.
+- [Open Questions from the Survey](../system-design.md#open-questions-from-the-survey) — the unresolved vet-cache question this decision defers to.
